[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94254"] :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-93726"] :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the third column must be orthogonal to the first and second columns, and\n\n- the norm of the third column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-94250/selection/070105025248e136bf99dd89a1b0be36f663aac25f74dfc0159770d0d382c421" :answer/id #uuid "f0d270e3-c061-5ce9-8328-37e01e5754fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94250/selection/e2d8ecb3ecc975c10f6fdd452b7a56add527d57a827ed1ed93b78a66c809c445" :answer/id #uuid "5526ee36-d500-5e9d-b0ac-53690377ce9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94250/selection/264bfdb050404d0528eb2df83076187125a528554a4408db8289bfe98ffbdc10" :answer/id #uuid "ec11834a-5238-58c3-895a-abb7ddf4cbcd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94250/selection/3d42d77764491e73541df274ce143d7a1aaea802736d20e1f8ece5616fa842b7" :answer/id #uuid "33dbfefd-5955-5981-80fa-c8047e2bb93e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94250/selection/151e6f91c06698042053ee074edfe3f28f3b45eb6aed07cad30e6482656d33b4" :answer/id #uuid "45d63311-498a-5087-8c38-f5f44cdde578" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{a